GL: the programs of a frame link side by side.
bgfx's GL backend read every shader's compile status right after glCompileShader and every program's link status
right after glLinkProgram, so the links of a frame ran one after another on the render thread even where the driver
compiles on threads of its own. The engine's lighting programs take 0.8 s each to link on an RTX 5070 Ti (NVIDIA
595.99, OpenGL ES 3.2, cold driver cache): a settings switch of ~33 programs held the title for 16 s. Now:
- with KHR_parallel_shader_compile the driver chooses its compiler threads (glMaxShaderCompilerThreadsKHR(~0));
  the backend logs whether it has them, and g_stormParallelProgramLink tells the engine, which then creates
  more programs per frame where no scene is shown (effects.cpp, Host::advance_switch);
- createShader only starts the compile; createProgram attaches the shaders and leaves the program for
  linkPendingPrograms, which at the start of submit() starts the links of all programs created by this frame's
  commands and only then reads their status (ProgramGL::finishLink: binary to the cache, init, detach);
- a shader's compile status is read when a program with it fails to link: a failed compile stays fatal;
- on Linux and Windows bgfx takes the GL entry points from the GLES library, which exports the core ones only
  (glvnd's libGLESv2): glMaxShaderCompilerThreadsKHR comes from eglGetProcAddress.
A program from the program cache (glProgramBinary) is ready at creation as before. Measured on the RTX 5070 Ti with
16 of the engine's lighting fragment shaders and their vertex shader: 15.8 s one after another, 1.8 s together.
Programs are destroyed only in cmdPost, after the draws, so none in the pending list is gone when it links.

